  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a3/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_53.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Tony Stark accidentally invents an antimatter ray, using an arrangement of circuitry that he claims he cannot remember, and is forced to admit that he couldn't reproduce even if he <i>wanted</i> to -- and shockingly, it <i>works</i>!&nbsp; Despite the fact that he now has no idea <i>how</i> it works, he elects to present it to the US Defense Department anyway.&nbsp; An antigravity force ray such as <i>this</i> is simply too important to hold back.\n\nHe presents it to the Pentagon, because it can be used to move large objects, such as massive, five-ton tanks.&nbsp; A photographer snaps a photo during the demonstration, and the news leaks, eventually making its way back to the Iron Curtain, where a Russian leader laments about the fact that stealing it would make a perfect mission for the Black Widow -- to get back in his good graces -- but she remains a fugitive.\n\nBut, unbeknownst to him, the Black Widow is <i>also</i> reading of the news, and she too views the theft of Stark's weapon as the <i>perfect</i> chance to show she is still a valuable asset to her Russian boss.&nbsp; She worries about the possibility of facing Iron Man again, but as for Stark, she views him as weak... and sympathetic... something she can use against him.&nbsp; She immediately scribbles down a \"tender little note\" to him, hoping that he's still interested in her (as we saw he was in last month's issue).\n\nThe note reaches Tony, who agrees to see Madame Natasha once again (much to Pepper's consternation).&nbsp; The Russian woman then apologizes for trying to harm him... <i>and Tony swallows her story</i>!&nbsp; He even shares a \"mistake\" of his <i>own</i> -- his antigravity ray, which he tells her is impossible to reproduce.&nbsp; He indicates it was a mistake for him to tell the government about it.\n\nAfter Tony produces the device and gives her a brief demonstration, she decides to steal it, viewing the theft as her ticket back home.&nbsp; A small bit of paralyzing spray is all it takes to immobilize Tony Stark and allow her to walk off with the device!&nbsp; Using its power allows her to eliminate all obstacles between her and the exit of the Stark factory grounds.\n\nOnce recovered from the effects of the paralyzing gas, Tony intimates that he knew <i>all along</i> that Black Widow's sudden turnaround was likely artificial, but he never expected her to move <i>so quickly</i>!&nbsp; It's now up to Iron Man to hunt her down!\n\nNatasha then contacts the Kremlin and promises to return, but only if she is \"forgiven.\"&nbsp; Her boss agrees, but only on the condition that she additionally eliminate both Stark and Iron Man.&nbsp; She enthusiastically consents, asserting that she \"never fails <i>twice</i>!\"\n\nIn the meantime, Stark has returned to his lab in an attempt to once again replicate his experiment, but with no success.&nbsp; It seems that it will require the power of Iron Man to stop her.\n\nBut while he is out hunting, the Black Widow begins to travel from factory to factory, creating havoc with the antigravity device, its power seemingly limitless, even able to lift entire <i>buildings</i> off of their foundations!&nbsp; Iron Man is forced to race from place to place, trying to repair the damage, leaving the Black Widow free to roam about, attacking Stark's production plants at will -- and all from a safe distance.\n\nFinally, some Russian cohorts arrive to help her and demand a demonstration of the device, but this only allows Iron Man to finally track down her location.&nbsp; In defense, she uses the mechanism to bring down a building on top of Iron Man, before fleeing to Fort Knox to steal its gold, as now ordered by her government.&nbsp; She actually aims the device at the mountain over Fort Knox, causing it to rise, as well as the tanks guarding it.\n\nWhen Iron Man finally does manage to nullify the weapon with a \"proton beam\" from his armor, the mountain begins to fall.&nbsp; He then takes a moment to rescue the Black Widow's cohorts from the plummeting rock, the sight of which makes Natasha wonder, \"What manner of men are these Americans, who risk their lives for their enemies?\"\n\nEven though the two cohorts are captured, the Black Widow slips away, but now with a weapon that is inoperative, for the antigravity ray is out of commission for good.&nbsp; Iron Man has saved the day.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "This is the very definition of a trying story, one that tests the patience of a reader.\n\n[ASSET: left | BlindChance | width=14%] [ASSET: right | SensationalNewWeapon | width=16%] There are so many plot points that are essentially shoved down the reader's throat, from Tony's creation of the antigravity ray by means of [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"blind chance,\"] to his decision to present it to the US Defense Department <i>anyway</i>, to the fact that its very existence is then <b>leaked out</b>, all because of some photographer, who apparently was allowed to wander around the Pentagon, to the final silliness of it making the <i>front pages</i>!\n\nIt was a veritable cavalcade of [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=\"You gotta be kidding me!\"] moments stacked on top of each other, and this, even <i>before</i> the Black Widow walks into the scene!\n\nBy the time she enters Tony's office under the pretense of apologizing for her actions last month, I was already checking the page count, ready for this lame excuse of a story to be over.\n[ASSET: center | Betrayed | width=large]\nSadly, there were still almost ten pages more to go!\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ChanceForRedemption | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: right | WreakingHavoc | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: left | SavingTheCommies | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\nThe Black Widow is still in full \"villain\" mode here, showing only a brief moment of wonder at the end that perhaps the Americans are more than her government has stated.&nbsp; Still not enough to make this a worthwhile read, not to mention that the cover image with the rays coming out of her hands makes it look like she has some kind of superpower -- very misleading.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nTo be somewhat fair, I want to at <i>least</i> acknowledge Don Heck's artwork, which was more than adequate.&nbsp; He has never been <i>outstanding</i>, but nevertheless continues to provide solid work that I belies [SPAN: severity=success; text=more than does the job] for this character, <i>especially </i>the many scenes involving Tony Stark.&nbsp; His finer lines ultimately make him a better choice for the strip (as a whole) than the overly-bombastic Jack Kirby, even if he doesn't have the same knack for the action sequences that \"King\" Kirby has.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nLet's be honest -- this was a very weak outing, filled with contrivances:\n[HANG] * An antigravity ray that <i>conveniently</i> cannot be reproduced,\n[HANG] * The front page news breaking about the <i>existence</i> of such a devastating weapon,\n[HANG] * The naive acceptance by Tony Stark of the Black Widow's \"regret\" over her previous actions,\n[HANG] * Tony not suspecting the danger she poses because <i>she's only a woman</i>,\n[HANG] * The silliness of pulling entire buildings from their foundations...\n[HANG] * ...and then massive stone rocks over Fort Knox,\n\nAnd to top it off, the cover by Jack Kirby was [SPAN: severity=error; text=horribly misleading,] showing mysterious rays coming out of the Black Widow's hands, making it look as if she has some kind of superpower, when in <i>reality</i>, she was only holding a small device, which she stole from Tony Stark.\n\nThe seemingly-endless parade of silliness essentially doomed the outing, and after this adventure, I'm left wondering exactly who the Black Widow is supposed to be.&nbsp; In her two appearances thus far, she strikes me as nothing more than a beautiful Russian spy.&nbsp; She may not be able to go back home due to her failure (as shown in the previous issue), but [SPAN: severity=warning; text=she certainly doesn't seem to have any future <i>here</i> in the United States!]\n\nI'm confused... exactly why are we supposed to think of her as some sort of threat?\n[BR] (<i>Surely</i>, it can't be because she can hold a small box and flip on its power switch!)\n[BR] Her usefulness to the Russians seems to have come to an end, so... should we expect to ever hear from her again?\n[BR] Her future seems to be <i>entirely</i> in flux...\n\n[HANG0] I suppose readers are expected to simply move on, because after all, as page 6 would have us believe:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=\"She <i>is</i> just a woman...!\"]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(shakes head -- looks like we have a contestant for this month's VMA)]\n\nOnly the backup tale that presented the Watcher's origin allowed this book to grade as high as it did.&nbsp; Even though that story itself it wasn't spectacular in its own right, it at least lifted part of the curtain to help explain why his race takes such a passive role in current events (or at least <i>purports</i> to -- when it comes to Uatu, it seems he doesn't always <i>succeed</i>).",
  "side_note": [
    "It's worth noting that this month's issue logo has a new look, one that emphasizes [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=The Power of IRON MAN] rather than it being just <i>Tales of Suspense</i>.&nbsp; Obviously, the title's <i>name</i> hasn't actually changed, but this marks the first domino to fall in the move to de-emphasize the old Atlas flavor of the books.",
    "<b>Tales of the Watcher:&nbsp; </b>\"The Way It Began...\"\n[BR] (by Stan Lee, Larry Lieber and Paul Reinman)\n\nAs the Watcher views an operation in a hospital, realizing he could tell them how to save the patient's life, he reflects on how his race chose to not interfere.&nbsp; They were once an advanced, peaceful race that lived in \"a distant galaxy\" with no crime or disease and had discovered the secret of immortality.&nbsp; In their generosity, they decided to share their \"vast knowledge with less fortunate races in the galaxy!\"&nbsp; One member disagrees, a named Emnu, but he yields to the will of the majority.\n\nSoon they travelled to a planet named Prosilicus, ready to help make them centuries more advanced.&nbsp; They leave them with the knowledge of such an advanced notion as \"nuclear energy\" and then head off to watch a \"multi-dimensional eclipse,\" a natural phenomenon that \"occurs only once every billion years.\"\n\nBut while they're gone, the people of Prosilicus, lacking the advanced race's superior <i>ethics</i>, begin to harness the nuclear power to fashion bombs and begin attacking each other.&nbsp; It isn't long before entire <i>cities</i> are being leveled and laid to waste.\n\nWhen the members of the Watcher's race finally return from the cosmic spectacle, they discover that, for Prosilicus, \"their entire civilization has been <i>wiped out</i>!\"&nbsp; The few that survive approach them and lay the blame squarely on their shoulders.&nbsp; \"You monsters!&nbsp; You gave us the secret of nuclear energy -- and that demoniacal force <i>destroyed</i> us!&nbsp; May you be cursed till the end of your days!\"\n\nEven Emnu is shocked, for he considered <i>himself</i> selfish, but it is the generosity of Uatu's father (Ikor) that led to this disaster.&nbsp; As penance for the catastrophe that his race participated in, Ikor vows \"that from this day on, my people will never interfere with any other being in the universe!&nbsp; We will become a race of <i>watchers</i> -- observing and recording, but never partaking in the affairs of others!\"\n\nUatu concludes:&nbsp; \"Thus have we ever been -- thus shall we ever be -- for such is our destiny...\""
  ],
  "ownership": "I was lucky enough to get a VG+ copy as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $13, and sold on eBay in Oct 2020 for $93, netting me over $68.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}